    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-123993",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{3}-ay)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-2x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{3}-ay,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-a & =3{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ -a & =-2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-2"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "1"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-\\frac{1}{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{2}"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "2",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-123993",
        "kp_title": "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-123999",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F=a\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y),\\,Q=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-a\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y).\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-a\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y)+a\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & =0 \\\\ (3+a)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & =0 \\\\ 3+a & =0 \\\\ a & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-3.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
