    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-104412",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\,i+x{e}^{x+y}\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,Q=x{e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(x+1){e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a){e}^{x+y} & =(x+1){e}^{x+y} \\\\ (x-a){e}^{x+y}-(x+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ (x-a-x-1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ -(a+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
