    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-123866",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j$\n- $G=2x\\,i-3(x+y)\\,j$\n- $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i+{e}^{y-x}\\,j$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x$ and $Q=-3(x+y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q={e}^{y-x}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{y-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
